<head>

. Git 
Use Git Bash or PowerShell.
1. Initialize and configure (4)
mkdir student-management
cd student-management
git init -b main
git config --global user.name "YourName"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
git config --list
2. Create files and commit (5)
echo "<h1>Home</h1>" > index.html
echo "body{}" > style.css
echo "console.log('hi');" > app.js
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
3. Branch feature-login and add a login form (5)
git checkout -b feature-login
echo "<form><input type='text'><input type='password'><button>Login</button></form>" >> index.html
git commit -am "Add login form"
4. Back to main, modify app.js (4)
git checkout main
echo "// main change" >> app.js
git commit -am "Modify app.js"
5. Merge feature-login into main (4)
git merge feature-login -m "Merge feature-login"
6. Create a conflict, resolve it, show the log (8)
git checkout -b feature-x
echo "body{color:red;}" > style.css
git commit -am "red"
git checkout main
echo "body{color:blue;}" > style.css
git commit -am "blue"
git merge feature-x
Expected: CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in style.css. Open the file with notepad style.css (or nano style.css in Git Bash). It shows:
<<<<<<< HEAD
body{color:blue;}
=======
body{color:red;}
>>>>>>> feature-x
Delete the three marker lines, keep one color line, save, then:
git add style.css
git commit -m "Resolve conflict"
git log --graph --oneline
            Q2. Jenkins Freestyle job (30 marks)
1. Install, start and log in (6)
• Open http://localhost:8080. If it does not open, run net start jenkins in an admin Command Prompt and wait 60 seconds.
• First-time password is in C:\ProgramData\Jenkins\.jenkins\secrets\initialAdminPassword.
• Click Install suggested plugins, create the admin user, click Start using Jenkins.
• If the installer says it cannot find Java 21 or 25, select the exact JDK folder, for example C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-21.0.12.1, not the parent Java folder.
Fix: Source Code Management shows only None. The Git plugin is missing. Go to Manage Jenkins, Plugins, Available plugins, search Git, tick Git, click Install, and tick restart when done. Also install Pipeline here for Q3.
2. Create the job (4)
New Item, name java-build-job (exactly this spelling), choose Freestyle project, OK.
3. Connect a Java project (6)
Source Code Management, select Git, URL https://github.com/jenkins-docs/simple-java-maven-app, branch */master (use */main if checkout fails). You can use your own repo if it is public and has a pom.xml in the root.
4. Trigger and build step (7)
• Build Triggers: tick Poll SCM, schedule H/2 * * * *
• Build Steps: Add build step, Execute Windows batch command, enter the two lines below (use your own JDK and Maven folders), then Save:
set JAVA_HOME=C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-21.0.12.1
"C:\Program Files\apache-maven-3.9.16\bin\mvn.cmd" clean package
Important: do not skip this step. A job with no build step also says Finished: SUCCESS, but nothing was built.
5. Run the build and show Console Output (7)
Build Now, open the latest build, Console Output. The end must show BUILD SUCCESS and Finished: SUCCESS.
Fixing a failed build (keep both screenshots)
• 'mvn' is not recognized: Jenkins runs as a service and cannot see your terminal PATH. Use the full paths in the build step as above, or add Maven bin and JAVA_HOME to the System variables and restart Jenkins (net stop jenkins, then net start jenkins).
• Checkout error: change */master to */main.
• no POM in this directory: the repo has no pom.xml in its root.
                      Q3. CI/CD Pipeline (30 marks)
Q2 must show BUILD SUCCESS first. Fork jenkins-docs/simple-java-maven-app on GitHub so you can commit to it.
1. Create the job (4)
New Item, name cicd-pipeline, choose Pipeline, OK. (If Pipeline is missing, install the Pipeline plugin.)
2. Jenkinsfile (16)
In the Pipeline section choose Pipeline script and paste this (replace YOUR_USER, and use main if that is your branch):
pipeline {
  agent any
  triggers { pollSCM('H/2 * * * *') }
  stages {
    stage('Checkout') { steps { git branch: 'master', url: 'https://github.com/YOUR_USER/simple-java-maven-app' } }
    stage('Build')    { steps { bat 'mvn clean compile' } }
    stage('Test')     { steps { bat 'mvn test' } }
    stage('Package')  { steps { bat 'mvn package' } }
    stage('Deploy')   { steps { bat 'if not exist deploy mkdir deploy & copy target\\*.jar deploy\\' } }
  }
}
Notes:
• \\ is not a comment. In Groovy it produces a single \ for Windows. Keep the double backslashes.
• This short version works only if Jenkins can see Maven and Java (same as Q2). If a stage says 'mvn' is not recognized, add this block after the triggers line, using your own folders:
  environment {
    JAVA_HOME = 'C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-21.0.12.1'
    PATH = "C:\\Program Files\\apache-maven-3.9.16\\bin;${env.PATH}"
  }
3. Run and show Stage View (5)
Build Now. The Stage View shows five green stages: Checkout, Build, Test, Package, Deploy. Open Workspace, then deploy to show the copied .jar.
4. Automatic trigger (5)
On GitHub edit README.md in your fork and commit. Do not click Build Now. Within 2 minutes build #2 starts by itself. Do step 3 first, because polling starts only after the first successful build.
Q4. Docker Basics (30 marks)
Start Docker Desktop (wait for Engine running). Stop Jenkins first: net stop jenkins (admin Command Prompt). Then use a normal PowerShell.
1. Pull nginx and run on port 8080 (6)
docker pull nginx
docker run -d --name web1 -p 8080:80 nginx
Browser: http://localhost:8080 shows Welcome to nginx.
2. Create the web app (5)
cd ~
mkdir myapp
cd myapp
Set-Content index.html "<h1>Hello Docker</h1>"
type index.html
If you get Access denied on mkdir, you are inside C:\WINDOWS\System32. Run cd ~ first.
3. Dockerfile (8)
Set-Content Dockerfile "FROM nginx:alpine","COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html"
type Dockerfile
4. Build my-web-app:v1 and run on port 8081 (6)
docker build -t my-web-app:v1 .
docker run -d --name web2 -p 8081:80 my-web-app:v1
Browser: http://localhost:8081 shows Hello Docker.
5. Management commands (5, compulsory)
docker ps
docker images
docker logs web2
docker stop web1 web2
docker rm web1 web2
docker ps -a
Run docker ps and docker images before stop and rm.
                                       Q5. Docker Compose (30 marks)
Normal PowerShell, Docker Desktop running.
1. Create the project (5)
cd ~
mkdir compose-app
cd compose-app
app.py
@'
from flask import Flask
import mysql.connector
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    c = mysql.connector.connect(host='db', user='root', password='root', database='testdb')
    cur = c.cursor()
    cur.execute("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users(id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50))")
    cur.execute("INSERT IGNORE INTO users VALUES (1,'Alice'),(2,'Bob')")
    c.commit()
    cur.execute("SELECT * FROM users")
    return str(cur.fetchall())

app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=80)
'@ | Set-Content app.py
Dockerfile
@'
FROM python:3.11-slim
RUN pip install flask mysql-connector-python
COPY app.py .
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
'@ | Set-Content Dockerfile
2. docker-compose.yml (12)
@'
services:
  web:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8000:80"
    depends_on:
      - db
  db:
    image: mysql:8.0
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: root
      MYSQL_DATABASE: testdb
    volumes:
      - db_data:/var/lib/mysql
volumes:
  db_data:
'@ | Set-Content docker-compose.yml
dir
3. Run and verify both containers (5)
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
4. Verify the app reads from the database (5)
Wait about 30 seconds for MySQL, then open http://localhost:8000. You see [(1, 'Alice'), (2, 'Bob')]. If you get an error, wait and refresh.
5. Scale down, logs, clean up (3)
docker compose up -d --scale web=0
docker compose logs
docker compose down -v
Quick troubleshooting
• Port already in use: stop Jenkins for Q4, or change the left side of the port mapping (for example 8001:80).
• Docker cannot connect: open Docker Desktop and wait for Engine running.
• Container name already in use: docker rm -f web1 web2.
• Jenkins job fails on Maven: use full paths in the build step (Q2) or the environment block (Q3).





Kubernetes Basics (Windows, minikube)
Step 0. Install Ubuntu (once)
Open PowerShell as Administrator and run wsl --install -d Ubuntu.
Restart the PC if it asks, then open Ubuntu from the Start menu and create a Linux username and password.
Turn on systemd so Apache can start as a service. In Ubuntu:
bash
echo -e "[boot]\nsystemd=true" | sudo tee /etc/wsl.conf
In PowerShell run wsl --shutdown, then reopen Ubuntu.

Run all the commands below inside the Ubuntu terminal, in your Linux home folder (cd ~), not in /mnt/c.

Step 1. Install Ansible and verify (4 marks)
bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y ansible
ansible --version

Screenshot the version output.

Step 2. Create the inventory and test the connection (5 marks)

Route B (one PC)

bash
mkdir ~/ansible && cd ~/ansible
printf "[webservers]\nlocalhost ansible_connection=local\n" > inventory.ini
ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping

Route A (2 servers)

bash
mkdir ~/ansible && cd ~/ansible
ssh-keygen                      # press Enter 3 times
ssh-copy-id user@NODE1_IP
ssh-copy-id user@NODE2_IP
printf "[webservers]\nNODE1_IP ansible_user=user\nNODE2_IP ansible_user=user\n" > inventory.ini
ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping

Screenshot it. You want "ping": "pong" for each host.

Step 3. Write the playbook (12 marks)
bash
echo "<h1>Hello from Ansible</h1>" > index.html

cat > install-packages.yml <<'EOF'
- hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  tasks:
    - apt:
        update_cache: yes
    - apt:
        name: [apache2, curl, git, vim]
        state: present
    - service:
        name: apache2
        state: started
        enabled: yes
    - copy:
        src: index.html
        dest: /var/www/html/index.html
EOF

Screenshot the file with cat install-packages.yml.

Step 4. Run the playbook (5 marks)
bash
ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini install-packages.yml --ask-become-pass

Enter your Linux password at the BECOME prompt. The recap should show failed=0. Screenshot it.

Step 5. Verify in the browser and prove idempotency (4 marks)
Open http://localhost in your Windows browser (Route A: http://NODE1_IP). You should see “Hello from Ansible”. Screenshot it.
Run the playbook again:
bash
ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini install-packages.yml --ask-become-pass
The recap should show changed=0. Screenshot it. This proves idempotency.




Use a normal PowerShell with Docker Desktop on Engine running. If your prompt shows System32, run cd ~ first.

Step 0. Install kubectl and minikube (once)
powershell
winget install Kubernetes.kubectl
winget install Kubernetes.minikube

Close PowerShell, open a new one, and check:

powershell
kubectl version --client
minikube version
Step 1. Start the cluster and check status (3 marks)
powershell
minikube start --driver=docker
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes

The first start takes a few minutes. The node should show Ready.

Step 2. Create a Pod (5 marks)
powershell
kubectl run my-pod --image=nginx
kubectl get pods

Run kubectl get pods again until my-pod shows Running.

Step 3. Create the Deployment from YAML (8 marks)
powershell
cd ~
@'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx
'@ | Set-Content deployment.yaml

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl get deployments

Screenshot the file (type deployment.yaml) and the created message.

Step 4. Create a NodePort Service and open it (8 marks)
powershell
kubectl expose deployment web-deployment --type=NodePort --port=80
kubectl get svc

Note the NodePort in the output (for example 80:31234/TCP). Then:

powershell
minikube service web-deployment --url

Copy the printed URL (for example http://127.0.0.1:52341) into the browser. You should see “Welcome to nginx!”.

Keep this PowerShell window open. On Windows with the Docker driver, closing it stops the tunnel. Open a second PowerShell window for Step 5.

Step 5. Show the outputs (6 marks)
powershell
kubectl get pods
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl describe pod my-pod

Screenshot each output.





Q8. Scaling, Rolling Updates and Rollbacks (Windows)

This uses the same minikube cluster as Q7. Use PowerShell with Docker Desktop running.

Step 0. Check the cluster and create my-app
powershell
minikube status
kubectl get deployments

If minikube is stopped, run minikube start --driver=docker. The question says my-app is already running, but if it’s missing, create it:

powershell
kubectl create deployment my-app --image=nginx:1.25 --replicas=2
kubectl get pods

Wait until both pods show Running.

Step 1. Scale from 2 to 5 replicas (5 marks)
powershell
kubectl scale deployment my-app --replicas=5
kubectl get pods
kubectl get deployment my-app

The deployment should show 5/5 ready. Screenshot it.

Step 2. Rolling update to nginx:1.27 (8 marks)
powershell
kubectl set image deployment/my-app nginx=nginx:1.27

Screenshot the output: deployment.apps/my-app image updated.

Step 3. Monitor the rollout (5 marks)
powershell
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app
kubectl get pods -w

The status command ends with successfully rolled out. Press Ctrl+C to stop the watch. To catch pods changing live, start kubectl get pods -w in a second PowerShell window just before Step 2.

Step 4. Check the rollout history (4 marks)
powershell
kubectl rollout history deployment/my-app

You should see revisions 1 and 2.

Step 5. Roll back and verify (8 marks)
powershell
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app
kubectl describe deployment my-app | Select-String Image

The last line should show Image: nginx:1.25. Screenshot it. PowerShell has no grep, so Select-String does the same job.





Q9. Centralized Logging with ELK (Windows)

ELK needs about 4 GB of free RAM. Stop minikube first with minikube stop, and close heavy apps. Use a normal PowerShell with Docker Desktop on Engine running.

Step 1. Start Elasticsearch, Logstash and Kibana (8 marks)
powershell
wsl -d docker-desktop sysctl -w vm.max_map_count=262144
cd ~
mkdir elk
cd elk
mkdir logs

docker-compose.yml

powershell
@'
services:
  elasticsearch:
    image: docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:8.13.4
    environment:
      - discovery.type=single-node
      - xpack.security.enabled=false
      - ES_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms512m -Xmx512m
    ports:
      - "9200:9200"
  logstash:
    image: docker.elastic.co/logstash/logstash:8.13.4
    volumes:
      - ./logstash.conf:/usr/share/logstash/pipeline/logstash.conf
      - ./logs:/logs
    ports:
      - "5044:5044"
    depends_on:
      - elasticsearch
  kibana:
    image: docker.elastic.co/kibana/kibana:8.13.4
    environment:
      - ELASTICSEARCH_HOSTS=http://elasticsearch:9200
    ports:
      - "5601:5601"
    depends_on:
      - elasticsearch
'@ | Set-Content docker-compose.yml

Create the Logstash config (Step 2) and the sample logs (Step 3) before starting, then run:

powershell
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps

Wait 1 to 2 minutes, then verify Elasticsearch on port 9200:

powershell
curl.exe localhost:9200

Screenshot the JSON output. You can also open http://localhost:9200 in the browser.

Step 2. Configure the Logstash pipeline (8 marks)
powershell
@'
input {
  beats { port => 5044 }
  file { path => "/logs/access.log" start_position => "beginning" sincedb_path => "/dev/null" }
}
filter {
  grok { match => { "message" => "%{COMMONAPACHELOG}" } ecs_compatibility => disabled }
}
output {
  elasticsearch { hosts => ["http://elasticsearch:9200"] index => "logs-%{+YYYY.MM.dd}" }
}
'@ | Set-Content logstash.conf

The pipeline takes input from Beats on port 5044 (and the log file), parses each line with grok, and sends the output to Elasticsearch. Screenshot the file with type logstash.conf.

Step 3. Generate sample logs (4 marks)
powershell
@'
127.0.0.1 - - [17/Nov/2026:10:00:01 +0000] "GET /index.html HTTP/1.1" 200 1024
127.0.0.1 - - [17/Nov/2026:10:00:02 +0000] "GET /login HTTP/1.1" 500 512
127.0.0.1 - - [17/Nov/2026:10:00:03 +0000] "GET /home HTTP/1.1" 200 2048
127.0.0.1 - - [17/Nov/2026:10:00:04 +0000] "POST /pay HTTP/1.1" 500 256
127.0.0.1 - - [17/Nov/2026:10:00:05 +0000] "GET /about HTTP/1.1" 200 900
'@ | Set-Content logs\access.log

After Logstash has started, confirm the data arrived:

powershell
curl.exe "localhost:9200/_cat/indices?v"

You should see an index like logs-2026.11.18 with 5 documents.

Step 4. Create the data view in Kibana (5 marks)
Open http://localhost:5601 (the first load can take a minute).
Open the menu, then Stack Management, then Data Views.
Click Create data view.
Name and index pattern: logs-*. Timestamp field: @timestamp.
Click Save data view to Kibana. Screenshot it.
Step 5. Show total, error and success logs (5 marks)
Open the menu, then Discover, and pick the logs-* data view.
Set the time range (top right) to Last 7 days.
The hit count at the top is the total log count (5). Screenshot it.
Search response:500. You should see 2 hits (error logs). Screenshot it.
Search response:200. You should see 3 hits (success logs). Screenshot it.

For an optional chart, go to Visualize Library, Create visualization, Lens, choose a pie chart, and split by response.keyword.

When done, run docker compose down.






Q10. Monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana (Windows)

Node Exporter is Linux-only, so on Windows you use windows_exporter, which does the same job. We start it on port 9100, so the config matches the question. Dashboard 1860 is for Linux, so you import 20763 instead.

If the lab PC is Linux, use node_exporter and dashboard 1860 as in the first guide.

Downloads
Prometheus: prometheus.io/download, choose the Windows .zip (windows-amd64).
windows_exporter: github.com/prometheus-community/windows_exporter/releases, download the .exe for amd64.
Grafana: grafana.com/grafana/download, choose Windows (the .msi installer, or the standalone .zip if you have no admin rights).

Unzip Prometheus into C:\prometheus and put the exporter .exe in C:\exporter. Rename the exe to windows_exporter.exe for simplicity.

Step 1. Install and start Prometheus (5 marks)
powershell
cd C:\prometheus
.\prometheus.exe --config.file=prometheus.yml

Leave this window open. If Windows Firewall asks, click Allow access. Open http://localhost:9090 in the browser. Screenshot it.

Step 2. Configure prometheus.yml (7 marks)

Stop Prometheus (Ctrl+C), then in a new PowerShell:

powershell
@'
global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
scrape_configs:
  - job_name: prometheus
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  - job_name: node
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
'@ | Set-Content C:\prometheus\prometheus.yml

Start Prometheus again in its own window:

powershell
cd C:\prometheus
.\prometheus.exe --config.file=prometheus.yml

Screenshot the file with type C:\prometheus\prometheus.yml.

Step 3. Run the exporter and check targets (5 marks)

In a new PowerShell window:

powershell
cd C:\exporter
.\windows_exporter.exe --web.listen-address=":9100"

Open http://localhost:9100/metrics to confirm it prints metrics. Then open http://localhost:9090, go to Status, then Targets. Both prometheus and node should show UP. Screenshot it. Keep all three windows open.

Step 4. Install Grafana and add the data source (5 marks)
Run the Grafana .msi and finish the install. It starts as a service.
Open http://localhost:3000 and log in with admin / admin, then set a new password.
Go to Connections, then Data sources, then Add data source, and choose Prometheus.
URL: http://localhost:9090
Click Save & test. It should say success. Screenshot it.
Step 5. Import the dashboard and show live graphs (8 marks)
Go to Dashboards, then New, then Import.
Enter 20763 and click Load.
Select the Prometheus data source and click Import.
Wait a minute for data, then set the time range to Last 15 minutes.
Screenshot the panels for CPU, Memory, Disk and Network.
